# Line endings.
#
# Everything here runs inside a Linux container: the entrypoint is a shell
# script, Compose parses the .env files, and the source tree is bind-mounted
# straight in. A CRLF checkout breaks all three, and none of them fail in a way
# that looks like a line-ending problem:
#
#   * docker-entrypoint.sh — the shebang becomes #!/usr/bin/env bash\r, so the
#     container exits with "no such file or directory" while the file is plainly
#     sitting there. Reads as a broken image.
#
#   * .env — Compose keeps the \r as part of the value, so POSTGRES_PASSWORD
#     gains a trailing carriage return and authentication fails against a
#     password that looks character-for-character correct on screen.
#
#   * anything run with docker compose exec.
#
# Windows git defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which produces exactly this unless
# a file like this one says otherwise. So: normalise to LF in the repository and
# in the working tree. Editors on Windows have handled LF for many years; the
# alternative is the three bugs above, each costing someone an afternoon.
text=auto eol=lf

# Stated explicitly for the files that fail loudest, so the intent survives
# someone editing the blanket rule above.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
Dockerfile.*    text eol=lf
.env            text eol=lf
.env.*          text eol=lf
Caddyfile       text eol=lf

# Binary — never normalise, never diff as text.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.webp   binary
*.pdf    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.zip    binary
*.gz     binary
*.parquet binary
*.pyc    binary

# Generated: keep them out of diffs and out of language statistics.
package-lock.json  linguist-generated=true
